## Findings
Initial revision: post-transcriptional regulation is plausible and context supported; no universal decay explanation is assumed. Preserve RBP counterexample and distinguish artificial AGO recruitment from endogenous miRNA loading.

Primary-source audit completed through JATS/BioC and the original Hollien PDF with supplement (inputs/primary). PMID19170179 is PMC2713384. Figures 5-7 establish seed-dependent reporter effects, endogenous RNA/protein response and transfected Ago2 association in newborn-rat Schwann cultures; no half-life. PMC6920087 has mouse WT/C22 Schwann RNA and human fibroblast RNA48h/protein72h, not matched rate measures.

IRE1 evidence is stronger than an association: PMC2728407 Table I labels Pmp22 decreased stability and refers to S2. The retrieved PDF S2 caption confirms actinomycin-D +/-DTT in hIre1R mouse fibroblasts, with technical qPCR error bars. Keep source-reported target-specific stability separate from pathway-specific RNase controls. A Pmp22-specific RNase-mutant decay interaction remains unverified. No curve digitization, visual-graph verification or half-life fitting is claimed. Final methods audit distinguishes 6h DTT microarrays from 5h qPCR validation.

Contradictions retained: PMC3866477 G3BP1 depletion increases RNA but has no significant half-life change in MCF-7 cells (Table1); this is not equivalence. Acute Dicer inhibition in cultured rat cells raises PMP22 protein, whereas developmental P0::Cre Dicer1 loss lowers Pmp22 and Egr2 in mouse nerves (PMC2906453). The latter has developmental arrest and residual miR29a, not a clean selective derepression test. Its rat miRNA developmental trend also differs from PMID19170179. PMC3124220 sustained rat Dicer shRNA lowers miR29a but impairs differentiation. No direct endogenous AGO-loaded miR29 rate series was found in these sources.

Newly audited human relevance: searched shared forum for 10545524, awad203 and UTR deletion before retrieving PMC10545524/PMID37337674 (Brain 2023). No prior result on that exact study returned. A human 650-bp UTR-deletion pedigree plus deletion reporter resistance to miR29a materially strengthens physiological UTR relevance beyond fibroblast transfer. Two biopsies are one proband, not two donors; the broad deletion includes other predicted miRNA sites; RT4/Oli-neu reporters are not primary human Schwann cells. This changes the starting position to human disease-associated UTR support, while decay fraction and miR29-specific endogenous necessity remain unidentified. This is recovered literature, not new biology.

Discovery of IGF2BP3/EDC3 study PMC13431160 led to metadata resolution of GSE289482 after forum searches for IGF2BP3/SLAM/accession found no overlap. Saved full CLI responses, file listing and sample metadata under inputs/geo. Before acquisition, selected control versus siIGF2BP3 design in HCT116 (not glia). Only one native processed sample was acquired for format inspection: GSM8792189, asset_47188fba5a4c009350afca180c0a45c4, blob78eeaf73be9d1576d1713c3a97b9988d386f713a21842f128eeb3f0a45f7d295. The preliminary 16-file selection is NOT a claim those files were all acquired or analyzed.

Rate-analysis stop decision: GSE289482 GEO says 4h tunicamycin/QuantSeq 3-prime libraries/250 micromolar s4U; article results/methods say 5h, methods NEBNext poly(A)/UltraII and Figure3 says 100 micromolar while methods say250. Native gzip is tab-delimited despite .csv name; gene_name entries are versioned RefSeq-like accessions, not gene symbols. No gene-level PMP22 absence or zero can be inferred. The two-hour pulse has no chase and no ERN1 RNase perturbation. IGF2BP3 association with IRE1 is not RIDD mediation; no quantitative PMP22 effect or half-life was calculated. Source outlier exclusion (siControl stress replicate3) is retained as metadata, not newly replicated. GSE118660 is total RNA time-course PERK WT/null MEF and NIH3T3, no same-sample RPF/chase in audited metadata; do not combine with inherited stress footprints. Upstream circRNA candidate PMC11784151 remains hypothetical; its mutant reporter is the circRNA sequence, not the PMP22 UTR.

